From fa9a7669fdae07e5a08f95450b2a4762f57ab7b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <you@example.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 01:49:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] BLWP: a stake lock accepted in ~78 ms (kanban #178): round
 delay 5 ms, the 10-year bound kept, send before count

Measured on a private 7-guardian regtest federation (5 of 7) on the MacBook, 20 locks: submit until the client knows
the federation accepted the lock p50 78 ms, p95 141 (with the lock client's changes in the BLWP repo).

- engine.rs: the exponential slowdown after a session's last round is the fewest rounds (at least 1000, as before) that
  still take 10 years at this guardian's round delay - the same sum aleph_bft checks. At 50 ms it stays 1000. The RAM
  bound grows by those few rounds. aleph_bft's member tick is a quarter of the round delay, 1-10 ms.
- api.rs submit_transaction: the transaction goes to consensus before the session's accepted items are counted (the
  count walks every item of the session; measured ~15-20 ms a lock). The session index is still read first, so the
  session-boundary resubmit is unchanged; after the count an already-accepted transaction is answered at once, so no
  acceptance is missed. Debug timing lines: validated, sent, counted.
- data_provider.rs: a debug line naming each transaction put into a unit.
- config: DEFAULT_BROADCAST_ROUND_DELAY_MS 50 -> 5. Only new local configs take it; running guardians keep theirs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FBa6xHgUpC65ky7yRpZgCQ
